            "B;point;lineSlider;C,1X,40,280,0",
            "N;point;planeSlider;floor,180,180,0",
            "M;point;lineSlider;C,1Y,150,190,160",
            "L;point;parallelogram;N,C,M",
            "A;point;parallelogram;B,C,M",
            "G;point;parallelogram;B,C,N",
            "K;point;parallelogram;G,C,M",
            "E;point;lineSlider;C,1X,230,250,0",
            "F;point;lineSlider;C,1X,305,250,0",
            "R;point;similar;E,F,floor,B,C,N,floor",
            "Q';point;parallelogram;M,C,F;0;0",
            "Q;point;proportion;B,C,E,F,C,M,F,Q'",
            "D;point;parallelogram;E,F,Q",
            "H;point;parallelogram;E,F,R",
            "P;point;parallelogram;R,F,Q",
            "O;point;parallelogram;H,F,Q",
            "ABCG;polyhedron;tetrahedron;A,B,C,G;0;0;black;random",
            "DEFH;polyhedron;tetrahedron;D,E,F,H;0;0;black;random",
            "GNLK;polygon;quadrilateral;G,N,L,K;0;0;black;0",
            "HRPO;polygon;quadrilateral;H,R,P,O;0;0;black;0",
            "KAML;polygon;quadrilateral;K,A,M,L;0;0;black;0",
            "ODQP;polygon;quadrilateral;O,D,Q,P;0;0;black;0",
            "MC;line;connect;M,C",
            "CN;line;connect;C,N",
            "QF;line;connect;Q,F",
            "FR;line;connect;F,R",
            // Transparent highlight targets: edges, bases, faces.
            "AB;line;connect;A,B;0;0;0",
            "BC;line;connect;B,C;0;0;0",
            "BG;line;connect;B,G;0;0;0",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E;0;0;0",
            "EF;line;connect;E,F;0;0;0",
            "EH;line;connect;E,H;0;0;0",
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;0;0",
            "DEF;polygon;triangle;D,E,F;0;0;0;0",
            "BM;polygon;quadrilateral;B,C,M,A;0;0;0;0",
            "BN;polygon;quadrilateral;B,C,N,G;0;0;0;0",
            "BK;polygon;quadrilateral;B,A,K,G;0;0;0;0",
            "CMLN;polygon;quadrilateral;C,M,L,N;0;0;0;0",
            "EQ;polygon;quadrilateral;E,F,Q,D;0;0;0;0",
            "ER;polygon;quadrilateral;E,F,R,H;0;0;0;0",
            "EO;polygon;quadrilateral;E,D,O,H;0;0;0;0",
            "FQPR;polygon;quadrilateral;F,Q,P,R;0;0;0;0",
            "KGN;polygon;triangle;K,G,N;0;0;0;0",
            "ACNK;polygon;quadrilateral;A,C,N,K;0;0;0;0",
            // Pyramid wireframes: each octagon walks the six edges.
            "pABCG;polygon;octagon;A,B,C,G,A,C,G,B;0;0;0;0",
            "pDEFH;polygon;octagon;D,E,F,H,D,F,H,E;0;0;0;0",
            "angABC;sector;angleMarker;B,A,C",
            "angGBC;sector;angleMarker;B,G,C",
            "angABG;sector;angleMarker;B,A,G",
            "angDEF;sector;angleMarker;E,D,F",
            "angHEF;sector;angleMarker;E,H,F",
            "angDEH;sector;angleMarker;E,D,H"
        ],
        deferDraggables: ["M","N"],
        aliases: { "MB": "BM" },
        slides: [
            { text: "“Similar pyramids with triangular bases are in triplicate ratio of their corresponding sides.”",
              visible: PYR,
              highlighted: ["pABCG","pDEFH"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "XII.8" }] },
            // Joyce's slip, left literal: "bases AB" for ABC.
            { text: "Let there be similar and similarly situated pyramids with triangular bases AB and {DEF} vertices {G} and {H}.",
              highlighted: ["pABCG","pDEFH","ABC","DEF","G","H"] },
            { text: "I say that the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} has to the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} the ratio triplicate of that which {BC} has to {EF}.",
              highlighted: ["pABCG","pDEFH","BC","EF"] },
            { text: "Complete the parallelepipedal solids BGML and EHQP.",
              visible: ALL,
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "MC", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "CN", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "KAML", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline },
                  { elem: "GNLK", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline },
                  { elem: "QF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "FR", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "ODQP", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline },
                  { elem: "HRPO", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outline } ] } },
            { text: "Now, since the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} is similar to the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH},",
              highlighted: ["pABCG","pDEFH"] },
            { text: "therefore the angle {ABC|angABC} equals the angle {DEF|angDEF},",
              visible: ANG,
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angABC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angDEF", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "the angle {GBC|angGBC} equals the angle {HEF|angHEF},",
              visible: ANG2,
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angGBC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angHEF", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "the angle {ABG|angABG} equals the angle {DEH|angDEH},",
              visible: ANG3,
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angABG", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angDEH", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "and {AB} is to {DE} as {BC} is to {EF}, and as {BG} is to {EH}.",
              highlighted: ["AB","DE","BC","EF","BG","EH"] },
            { text: "And since {AB} is to {DE} as {BC} is to {EF}, and the sides are proportional about equal angles,",
              highlighted: ["AB","DE","BC","EF","angABC","angDEF"] },
            { text: "therefore the parallelogram {BM} is similar to the parallelogram {EQ}.",
              highlighted: ["BM","EQ"] },
            // Joyce's slip, left literal: "BR" for BK.
            { text: "For the same reason {BN} is also similar to {ER}, and BR similar to {EO}.",
              highlighted: ["BN","ER","BK","EO"] },
            { text: "Therefore the three parallelograms {MB}, {BK}, and {BN} are similar to the three {EQ}, {EO}, and {ER}.",
              highlighted: ["BM","BK","BN","EQ","EO","ER"] },
            { text: "But the three parallelograms {MB}, {BK}, and {BN} are equal and similar to their three opposites,",
              highlighted: BOX1,
              justifications: [{ claim: "opposite planes equal and similar", ref: "XI.24" }] },
            { text: "and the three {EQ}, {EO}, and {ER} are equal and similar to their three opposites.",
              highlighted: BOX2,
              justifications: [{ claim: "opposite planes equal and similar", ref: "XI.24" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the solids BGML and EHQP are contained by similar planes equal in multitude.",
              highlighted: BOX1.concat(BOX2) },
            { text: "Therefore the solid BGML is similar to the solid EHQP.",
              highlighted: BOX1.concat(BOX2) },
            { text: "But similar parallelepipedal solids are in the triplicate ratio of their corresponding sides.",
              highlighted: BOX1.concat(BOX2) },
            { text: "Therefore the solid BGML has to the solid EHQP the ratio triplicate",
              highlighted: BOX1.concat(BOX2) },
            { text: "of that which the corresponding side {BC} has to the corresponding side {EF}.",
              highlighted: ["BC","EF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "solids in triplicate ratio of BC to EF", ref: "XI.33" }] },
            { text: "But the solid BGML is to the solid EHQP as the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} is to the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH},",
              highlighted: ["pABCG","pDEFH"].concat(BOX1, BOX2) },
            { text: "for the pyramid is a sixth part of the solid,",
              highlighted: ["pABCG"].concat(BOX1) },
            { text: "because the prism which is half of the parallelepipedal solid is also triple the pyramid.",
              visible: FAC,
              highlighted: PRISM.concat(["pABCG"]),
              justifications: [{ claim: "the prism is half the solid", ref: "XI.28" },
                               { claim: "the prism is triple the pyramid", ref: "XII.7" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} has to the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} the ratio triplicate of that which {BC} has to {EF}.",
              visible: ANG3,
              highlighted: ["pABCG","pDEFH","BC","EF"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "XII.8") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "XI.24": "/elements/books/bookXI/propositions/propXI24/",
        "XI.28": "/elements/books/bookXI/propositions/propXI28/",
        "XI.33": "/elements/books/bookXI/propositions/propXI33/",
        "XII.7": "/elements/books/bookXII/propositions/propXII7/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Now, since the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} is similar to the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH}, therefore the angle {ABC|angABC} equals the angle {DEF|angDEF}, the angle {GBC|angGBC} equals the angle {HEF|angHEF}, the angle {ABG|angABG} equals the angle {DEH|angDEH}, and {AB} is to {DE} as {BC} is to {EF}, and as {BG} is to {EH}.

And since {AB} is to {DE} as {BC} is to {EF}, and the sides are proportional about equal angles, therefore the parallelogram {BM} is similar to the parallelogram {EQ}. For the same reason {BN} is also similar to {ER}, and *BR* similar to {EO}.

Therefore the three parallelograms {MB}, {BK}, and {BN} are similar to the three {EQ}, {EO}, and {ER}. But the three parallelograms {MB}, {BK}, and {BN} are equal and similar to their three opposites, and the three {EQ}, {EO}, and {ER} are equal and similar to their three opposites. [!just XI.24]

Therefore the solids *BGML* and *EHQP* are contained by similar planes equal in multitude. Therefore the solid *BGML* is similar to the solid *EHQP.*

But similar parallelepipedal solids are in the triplicate ratio of their corresponding sides. Therefore the solid *BGML* has to the solid *EHQP* the ratio triplicate of that which the corresponding side {BC} has to the corresponding side {EF}. [!just XI.33]

But the solid *BGML* is to the solid *EHQP* as the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} is to the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH}, for the pyramid is a sixth part of the solid, because the prism which is half of the parallelepipedal solid is also triple the pyramid. Therefore the pyramid {ABCG|pABCG} has to the pyramid {DEFH|pDEFH} the ratio triplicate of that which {BC} has to {EF}. [!just XI.28; XII.7]

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
<br clear="all">

<br>

<a name="cor"></a>

### Corollary

From this it is clear that similar pyramids with polygonal bases are also to one another in the triplicate ratio of their corresponding sides.

For, if they are divided into the pyramids contained in them which have triangular bases, by virtue of the fact that the similar polygons forming their bases are also divided into similar triangles equal in multitude and corresponding to the wholes, then the one pyramid with a triangular base in the one complete pyramid is to the one pyramid with a triangular base in the other complete pyramid as all the pyramids with triangular bases contained in the one pyramid is to all the pyramids with triangular bases contained in the other pyramid, that is, the pyramid itself with a polygonal base, to the pyramid with a polygonal base. [!just VI.20; V.12]

But the pyramid with a triangular base is to the pyramid with a triangular base in the triplicate ratio of the corresponding sides, therefore also the pyramid with a polygonal base has to the pyramid with a similar base the ratio triplicate of that which the side has to the side.

Therefore, *similar pyramids with triangular bases are in triplicate ratio of their corresponding sides.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
<br clear="all">
----
guide:

Heath gives a good argument that the corollary was added later.

#### Use of this proposition

This proposition is used to show similar cones are in triplicate ratio of the diameters of their bases in proposition @XII.12. Also, the corollary justifies a statement in the [corollary](/elements/books/bookXII/propositions/propXII17/#cor) of XII.17 concerning similar solids.
